---
/**
 * The zero-install tour — hub (HAUSSMANN P2.5 O1, ⛩ operator pick 2026-08-19: variant A).
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. The best-disposed reader in the cold-read packet — a senior engineer who
 * liked the licence and half-believed the pitch — refused the install, and then wrote down the
 * route he wanted instead: read CLAUDE.md and .adna/ raw in the browser, and only then decide.
 * He improvised that route because the site did not offer one. This page is his route, made
 * first-class. It is not a persuasion surface; it is an audit surface, and it should read like one.
 *
 * The files are vendored verbatim at a pinned commit by scripts/build_tour_files.mjs — see that
 * file for why they are .txt and not MDX, and why the pin is derived here rather than borrowed
 * from install_truth.json.
 */
import DocumentationLayout from '../../../layouts/DocumentationLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import manifest from '../../../data/tour_manifest.json';
import { TOUR_ANNOTATIONS } from '../../../data/tour_annotations';
import { sourceForTour } from '../../../utils/contentSource';
import { buildTechArticleJsonLD, buildBreadcrumbListJsonLD } from '../../../utils/seo';
import { SITE_ORIGIN } from '../../../data/canonical';

// Fail the build rather than render a tour with a missing annotation: a card with no "what to look
// for" is exactly the bare-generated-copy failure the campaign's editorial gate exists to catch.
const files = manifest.files.map((f) => {
  const note = TOUR_ANNOTATIONS[f.slug];
  if (!note) throw new Error(`tour file "${f.slug}" has no annotation in tour_annotations.ts`);
  return { ...f, note };
});

const siteBase = Astro.site?.toString().replace(/\/$/, '') ?? SITE_ORIGIN;
const totalKb = (manifest.files.reduce((n, f) => n + f.bytes, 0) / 1024).toFixed(0);

const title = 'What your agent reads';
const description =
  'Selected instruction files from a pinned aDNA release, with the actual workspace router linked separately. Inspect the sources before running an agent.';

const jsonLD = [
  buildTechArticleJsonLD({ title, description, url: Astro.url.href }),
  buildBreadcrumbListJsonLD([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${siteBase}/` },
    { name: 'Get Started', url: `${siteBase}/get-started/` },
    { name: title, url: `${siteBase}/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/` },
  ]),
];
---

<DocumentationLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  jsonLD={jsonLD}
  source={sourceForTour(`${manifest.source_repo}/tree/${manifest.source_ref}/.adna`)}
  prev={{ label: 'Get Started', href: '/get-started/' }}
>
  <p>
    aDNA is Markdown instructions that an agent reads and acts on. That is the whole mechanism, and
    it is also the reason you might not want to run our install command on a stranger's say-so:
    the files <em>are</em> the program.
  </p>
  <p>
    Inspect the sources first. Below are {files.length} selected files, about {totalKb}&thinsp;KB,
    from the pinned release. They are verbatim copies, not the complete set an agent may read.
    The router template is different from the ready-made root router in a fresh clone.
    <a href={`${manifest.source_repo}/blob/${manifest.source_ref}/CLAUDE.md`}>Read that release’s actual root CLAUDE.md</a>,
    or <a href={`${manifest.source_repo}/blob/main/CLAUDE.md`}>inspect the current clone’s router</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="Where these came from">
    <p>
      Vendored from <a href={`${manifest.source_repo}/tree/${manifest.source_ref}/.adna`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      the standard</a> at release <code>{manifest.source_ref}</code> ({manifest.source_commit_date}). The build
      checks these copies against their recorded hashes. A normal clone follows the repository’s
      default branch and may contain a newer version. Compare at this release, not against an
      assumed match with the latest clone.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="reading-order">The reading order</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the actual root router linked above. These selected files then explain governance,
    project creation and onboarding; the files an agent reads depend on its task and your workspace.
  </p>

  <ol class="tour-list">
    {files.map((f, i) => (
      <li class="tour-item">
        <div class="tour-index" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</div>
        <div class="tour-body">
          <h3 id={f.slug}>
            <a href={`/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/${f.slug}/`}>{f.note.title}</a>
          </h3>
          <p class="tour-path"><code>{f.source_path}</code> · {f.lines.toLocaleString()} lines</p>
          <p>{f.note.what}</p>
          <p><strong>Why your agent reads it.</strong> {f.note.why}</p>
          <p><strong>What to look for.</strong> {f.note.lookFor}</p>
          <p>
            <a href={`/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/${f.slug}/`}>Read {f.source_path.split('/').pop()} in full →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <h2 id="what-a-project-looks-like">And what it builds</h2>
  <p>
    These instructions describe how to create a project with an explicit structure. Here is a real one — the triad of this vault, the context graph that publishes
    the page you are reading:
  </p>
  <div class="tour-triad">
    {manifest.vault_triad.map((leg) => (
      <div class="tour-leg">
        <h3 id={`triad-${leg.leg}`}><code>{leg.leg}/</code></h3>
        <ul>{leg.entries.map((e) => <li><code>{e}/</code></li>)}</ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="tour-triad-note">
    Recorded from this repository when the tour data was generated. Knowledge in <code>what/</code>, operations in <code>how/</code>, people in
    <code>who/</code> — that split is the standard's central organizing convention, and everything above is in
    service of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="then-what">Then what</h2>
  <p>
    If the files look sane, the <a href="/get-started/">install page</a> states what the command
    writes, what the agent does with it, and how to undo it. If they do not, you have lost a few
    minutes of reading instead of an evening — which was the point of putting them here.
  </p>
</DocumentationLayout>

<style>
  /* Numbered cards. The list is semantic (<ol>) so screen readers get the ordering for free; the
     visible numeral is aria-hidden to avoid reading each position twice. */
  .tour-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
  }
  .tour-item {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-5) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .tour-item:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .tour-index {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .tour-body {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .tour-body > h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .tour-path {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: calc(var(--space-1) * -1);
  }
  .tour-path code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .tour-triad {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }
  .tour-leg {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--color-bg-alt);
  }
  .tour-leg > h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .tour-leg ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .tour-triad-note {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
</style>
